# Project Summary
## FMCG Sales & Profitability Analytics

**18,240 orders · 17 countries · 3 years (2023–2025)**
**Tools: BigQuery SQL · Tableau · Python**

---

## The Core Question

> Where does profit actually come from —
> and where is the business silently losing money?

---

## How We Answered It

Each tool answered a different layer of the same question:

```
SQL      - found the patterns
           "Online ROI = $0.68, Beverages margin = 12–17%"

Tableau  - made patterns visible and actionable
           Interactive dashboards for stakeholder decisions

Python   - proved patterns are statistically real
           and revealed the root cause
```

This cross-tool approach mirrors real analytics teams
where SQL, BI, and data science work the same problem
from different angles - and either confirm or challenge
each other's findings.

---

## Five Key Findings

---

### Finding 1 — COGS is the #1 profit driver

**Confirmed independently by all three tools:**

| Tool | Evidence |
|------|----------|
| SQL Q6 | COGS = 49–53% of gross sales across all channels |
| SQL Q5 | B2C COGS share 52.65% > B2B 49.59% |
| Python ML | COGS feature importance = 75.8% of profit variance |

Top 2 features (COGS + Product Category) explain **87.2%**
of all profit variation. Everything else — discounts,
channel, promotions, seasonality — explains only 12.8%.

**What this means for the business:**
Optimising discount levels or promotional mechanics
will not meaningfully move profitability.
The primary lever is supplier cost negotiation.

**Action:** Renegotiate supplier contracts.
3% COGS reduction across all categories
≈ **+$500K additional annual profit.**

---

### Finding 2 — Product category matters 76× more than channel

| Tool | Evidence |
|------|----------|
| SQL Q8 | Personal Care margin 27–30% vs Beverages 12–17% |
| SQL Q6 | Channel margin spread: 12.78%–26.48% |
| Python ML | Category importance 0.1138 vs Channel 0.0015 |

A sales rep selling Personal Care through Online
outperforms a rep selling Beverages through Wholesale.
**What you sell matters more than where you sell it.**

**Action:** Shift sales mix toward Personal Care
and Household. Adjust sales team incentives to reflect
category margin contribution, not just revenue volume.

---

### Finding 3 — Promotions reduce profit across every segment

**Confirmed by all three tools:**

| Tool | Evidence |
|------|----------|
| Marketing ROI | No Promo ROI $3.17 vs Festival Campaign $1.23 |
| A/B Test | Median gap = $29.08/order (p = 6.57e-53) |
| Python ML | is_promo importance = 0.002 (<1% of variance) |

Promotions are statistically confirmed to reduce profit
(A/B test) but are not the main driver of variance (ML).
The nuance: **promotions are a symptom, not the disease.**
The disease is COGS.

**Per-channel A/B results:**

| Channel | Effect (Cohen's d) | Verdict |
|---------|-------------------|---------|
| Online | 0.450 (medium) | Most damaged |
| Wholesale | 0.357 (small) | Significant |
| Modern Trade | 0.351 (small) | Significant |
| Distributor | 0.326 (small) | Significant |

**Only worthwhile campaign: Loyalty Cashback** (ROI $3.09)
— closest to No Promo performance, lowest loss rate (2.15%).

**Action:** Eliminate Festival Campaign immediately.
Shift freed budget to Loyalty Cashback.
Zero promotional spend on Online and Beverages.

**Estimated impact:** $273K–$404K annual profit gain
(Bootstrap 95% CI: $23.76–$35.18 per order × 11,499 promo orders).

