### Finding 4 — B2B outperforms B2C on 8 out of 9 metrics

| Metric | B2B | B2C | Gap |
|--------|-----|-----|-----|
| Total profit | $2.67M | $636K | 4.2× |
| Avg margin | 23.69% | 16.01% | +7.68pp |
| Marketing/profit ratio | 39.3% | 80.69% | 2× more expensive |
| Loss rate | 1.4% | 7.3% | 5× higher risk |
| Avg order value | $1,432 | $435 | 3.3× larger |

B2C spends **80 cents of marketing per $1 of profit.**
B2B spends 39 cents — twice as efficient.

**Important nuance:** B2C should not be abandoned.
It provides market breadth, brand visibility, and a pipeline
for potential B2B conversion. Goal: optimise, not eliminate.

**Action:** Protect B2B as priority segment.
Investigate B2C 7.3% loss rate by category and channel.
Set minimum margin floor for B2C orders.

---

### Finding 5 — Beverages is structurally unprofitable

The only finding confirmed strongly by all three tools
**in the same direction:**

| Tool | Evidence |
|------|----------|
| SQL Q8 | Margin 12–17%, no month is safe for promotion |
| A/B Test | Strongest promo damage by category (d = 0.290) |
| Python ML | Worst predicted category for Q1 2026 |

No Promo Beverages median = $79.47.
Promo Beverages median = $44.69.
**Promotions cut Beverages profit by 44%.**

Unlike seasonal underperformance (which can be timed around),
Beverages underperforms consistently regardless of month,
channel, promotion type, or customer segment.

**Three options:**

| Option | Action | Condition |
|--------|--------|-----------|
| A | Reprice + 8–10% | If demand is price-inelastic |
| B | Remove lowest-margin SKUs | If assortment can be reduced |
| C | Restrict to Wholesale/Distributor only | If channel mix drives losses |

